-- title:
--   Ratios of forms are integral over ℂ[j]
-- statement:
--   Let $M\ge 1$ be a natural number, let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ containing the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_1(M)$, and let $k$ be an integer. Let $g$ and $h$ be modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, with $h\neq 0$. Assume that for every point $\tau$ of the upper half-plane the function $z\mapsto \|g(z)/h(z)\|$ is bounded above along the filter of punctured neighbourhoods of $\tau$, i.e. $g/h$ is locally bounded near each point of $\mathbb{H}$ (punctures allowed). Form the $q$-expansions of $g$ and of $h$ with respect to the width $1$, viewed as Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$ via the inclusion of power series into `LaurentSeries ℂ`, and let $j$ denote the Laurent series $\mathrm{jqModC}\,\mathbb{C}$, namely $q^{-1}$ times the base change to $\mathbb{C}$ of the integral power series $\mathrm{jNum}=E_4^{3}\cdot(\text{the unit inverse of the Dedekind }\eta\text{ factor})$; by the cited identity this is $(\text{$q$-expansion of }E_4)^3/(\text{$q$-expansion of }\Delta)$. The conclusion is that the quotient of the $q$-expansion of $g$ by that of $h$, an element of $\mathbb{C}((q))$, is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{C}[j]$ of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by $j$: it satisfies a monic polynomial equation with coefficients in $\mathbb{C}[j]$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that a modular function for a congruence subgroup which is holomorphic on the upper half-plane is integral over $\mathbb{C}[j]$, here in the form of an integrality assertion about the $q$-expansion of such a function, obtained from the level-one case by multiplying by a power of $\Delta$. It feeds into the estimate [`ModularCurve.ord_sub_algebraMap_le_one_laurentBaseChange_qExpFunctionFieldC_of_ne_zero_of_ne_1728`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ord_sub_algebraMap_le_one_laurentBaseChange_qExpFunctionFieldC_of_ne_zero_of_ne_1728), which controls orders of vanishing of elements of the function field of the modular curve.
-- source:
